  "id": "Compound::DB00539 - Disease::DOID:1612",
  "verbalization": "Toremifene is implicated in the treatment of breast cancer through multiple pathways, primarily via its role in modulating estrogenic effects. The evidence suggests that Toremifene exerts an oestrogenic effect, which is mediated by compounds such as Anastrozole and Letrozole, both of which are known to act as antioestrogens. These compounds are linked to the treatment of breast cancer, indicating a potential therapeutic role for Toremifene in this context. The shared lowest common ancestors, such as Antiestrogen and Organonitrogen Compound, suggest a common biological mechanism in the context of estrogen receptor modulation.\n\nThe evidence also highlights the involvement of Toremifene in the treatment of breast cancer through a different pathway, involving Tamoxifen, which is a selective estrogen receptor modulator. This pathway underscores the broader role of Toremifene in influencing estrogen-related processes that are relevant to breast cancer. The integration of these pathways suggests that Toremifene may contribute to the treatment of breast cancer through mechanisms involving estrogen receptor modulation and the regulation of oestrogenic effects. The strength of the evidence is supported by the consistent scores across different paths, indicating a plausible relationship between Toremifene and breast cancer treatment.",
